amina muhammad abdi was known for being  a fearless and outspoken critic of  somalia's government until she was  killed two months ago  she was an opposition mp vying for a  third term  her sister samira says she wanted to be  part of a new government that would make  somalia a fairer and safer place  a suicide bomber ran at her at an  election meeting in the town of  belladoin  the armed group al-shabab said it was  responsible  she's one of hundreds of people who've  been killed in the 10th lead up to  somalia's presidential election which  has been delayed by more than a year  [Music]  samira says she lost much more than her  sister  amina always helped everyone who came  her way whether they were her relatives  or not or from any clan she didn't care  about those things she just saw people  and humanity if anyone was in need she  just cared about the needs of that  person  since amina was killed nearly 300 mps  have been sworn in  the somalis didn't get to vote for them  they were selected by clan  representatives and officials in  processes that many say were corrupted  and rigged  mps elect the president under a system  that was introduced after the central  government collapsed in 1991.  everyone familiar with the process says  buying of votes is widespread  winning depends on forging alliances  between factions  in the days leading up to the vote many  of the deals have been discussed here at  the heavily fortified jazeera hotel in  the capital mogadishu  it's a place where politicians and their  supporters can meet in relative safety a  televised debate between four of the  most prominent candidates was planned  advertised on this billboard but it  didn't happen the candidates disagreed  on the terms and so somalis haven't even  got to hear what the policies of the  different candidates are but what people  think won't affect the outcome because  the decision lies in the hands of the  members of parliament  people familiar with the process say the  large amounts of cash involved still  don't guarantee anyone a victory or even  a vote no candidate is campaigning  without money  then  all mbs are just roaming trafficking  around telling them i vote for you and  everyone is guaranteeing voting for you  for you for you someone can make money  from five six candidates and then you  have only one vote  people here hope that completing the  elections will make somalia a little  safer  but this is a place where politicians  can't even safely move around their own  capital city  whoever wins the vote faces some  enormous challenges malcolm webb al  jazeera mogadishu somalia
 

